{"author":{"id":"7674679dac9170669f752d932f8b5955b1d55717359e13b1c1e687003c7bb99f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794113873,"nonce":"jX9rLUJA+ISUPk4g/zwoOpP1PZ0=","title":"In a text, `esc` opens the palette and `esc esc` leaves","message":"## Design\n\n**Aspects changed:** `doc/design/views.md` (\"The comment box\", \"Editing the description\", \"The creator page\"), `doc/design/tui.md` (the palette, \"`esc x` is `alt+x`\").\n\n- **In the three boxes** (show's comment box, show's description editor, new's description box), `esc` opens the palette over the text, and the text stays focused underneath. A second `esc` leaves the text, **keeping the draft**: `space` goes back in, and leaving the page with a draft still asks for a second `esc`. This is the page's own rule (one `esc` opens the palette, two go back), so `esc` means the same thing in the text and outside it.\n- **From a text, the palette offers only the text's commands**: today `e` (edit in $EDITOR), plus `g` to close, which returns to the text. Any other letter rings. Not the full palette: a page command would have to leave the text first, a second thing for one key to do.\n- **No collisions:** a text command's letter is never one of the textarea's `alt` keys (`alt+f/b/d/c/l/u`, and any it adds). A test checks every text command's letter against the textarea's key map, so `alt+\u003cletter\u003e` and `esc \u003cletter\u003e` are one key in the text too. Other `alt` letters in the text stay the textarea's word keys.\n- **The slow-`esc` flag (`escaped` on show and new, from 514eb0d0) is removed**, and its palette quirk with it: `esc e` is the palette's `e`, as anywhere else.\n- **Unchanged:** one-line inputs (the title, picker filters, the list filter, the query editor) keep `esc` as clear or cancel; `enter` still sends, writes, or accepts (new).\n\nDesign approved by Luis on 2026-11-07.","files":null,"fields":{"area":["tui"],"priority":"medium","status":"in-progress","type":"story"}}]}